WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.020
Jay Armstrong: I took all the pills that I had...
I took 'em all.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.140
Jay Armstrong: Hi, my name is Jay Armstrong.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.680
Matt Peterson: My name is Matt Peterson

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.900
Justin Ponton: Justin Ponton.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.300
Kelvin Young: My
name is Kelvin Young.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.480
Justin Ponton: The first time I'd

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.980
ever used any substance at all I drank
with a couple of friends of mine. And I

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:35.640
tried marijuana the same time. The
heavy substance abuse really happened at

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.460
the age of 21.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.060
Sally Satel: Prescription opioid abuse
is a lot more complicated than the media

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.360
and politicians make it out to be.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.640
Jay Butler: Too often, sports-related injuries managed

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.440
with opioid pain relievers have been the
beginning of a path to addiction.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.890
Sally Satel: It's true that some people who get
prescription opioids from their doctors

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:01.350
do become addicted to them. But the
majority of people who abuse opioid

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:06.400
painkillers typically get them from
their friends, relatives.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.340
Jay Armstrong: So I'm 14, 15, 16 years old,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.360
and the guys I'm hanging out
with they're all 21 to 30 years old. At

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.939
one point they broke out some Dilaudids,
which is a pharmaceutical opiate that

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:25.590
you can break down and inject. And it
took me to the same place mentally and

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.689
emotionally that alcohol did. But I
didn't, you know, try to fight anybody. I

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:34.590
didn't puke, I didn't pee my pants, I
didn't try to take my clothes off in

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:39.420
public or any of the other crazy stuff
that I was doing with alcohol. And you

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.530
know, at the time it was like a miracle!
Like, oh my god, like why didn't we find

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.740
this sooner?

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.479
Sally Satel: The fact is that many of
these individuals already have

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:55.960
experience abusing drugs, from alcohol to
marijuana to inhalants to drugs like valium.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:02.070
Kelvin Young: For me I started drinking when I
was about 13, 14 years old.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:09.960
So by the time I reached age 26, 27, I
"graduated," so to speak,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.100
to cocaine and heroin.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.680
Matt Peterson: So at 13 I started
drinking and smoking pot, and then...

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:22.700
probably about two years later, cocaine
was introduced. The first time I abused

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:28.620
opiate painkillers, I had my wisdom
teeth taken out and my friends they

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.160
taught me how to crush up a pill and
then snort it.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.920
Justin Ponton: I started selling prescription pills.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.200
I was selling cocaine,
crack, heroin, weed... anything I could

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.380
pretty much get my hands on. And then I
started using the drugs myself.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:56.060
Sally Satel: At least half of all individuals whose overdose deaths are attributed to an opioid also

00:02:56.070 --> 00:03:01.260
have some other drug in their system at
death. And it's these drugs in

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.280
combination that are especially deadly.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.360
Jay Armstrong: I didn't plan on living another second, so
I didn't try to go to a place where I

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.600
could hide. I took all the pills that I
had. I took 'em all. And I did all the

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.760
heroin that I got, because I know mixing
benzos with opiates does the trick, you

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.180
know... and it should have done the
trick.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:38.069
Sally Satel: People who use drugs of all kinds are people who are trying to medicate some

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:41.939
sort of deep inner distress. It doesn't
mean they're bad people. It means they're

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:46.469
desperately looking for a way out and
can't figure out anything but drugs.

00:03:46.469 --> 00:03:52.859
Justin Ponton: A lot of the reasons why I did initially
begin to use substances was a lot due to

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.280
low self esteem, no sense of self-worth
and insecurities.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.100
Jay Armstrong: The theme of my childhood is that

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.720
I didn't feel wanted, I
didn't feel like I belonged, and I felt afraid.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:09.080
Kelvin Young: Growing up as the youngest
of five... I felt different. I felt

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:15.410
less than. When I did heroin... I felt good.
You know, it numbed that feeling of...

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.880
feeling less than, inadequate.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.400
Matt Peterson: Me growing
up as an only child, the son of a pastor...

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:27.500
I just wanted to fit in. But when I smoked pot
and drank for the first time, I felt felt accepted.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.400
Sally Satel: It's true that doctors have
over-prescribed opioids in the past and

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:41.580
now they've been cutting back since
around 2011 and this is a good

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:46.620
development. However, what doctors do in
their office will have no effect on

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:51.630
people who never got the prescription
opioids from their doctors and it also

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:59.300
has unintended consequences for patients
who truly are in pain and need those medications.

